//but over large timescales on the order of millions of years...then the expansiomn of the universe can't be neglected //

we know animals eg dinosaurs were much larger 200M+ years ago and have shrunk over time. I would  question whether the expansion of the universe has had an on impact on this which suggests the answer lies elsewhere.

The example of throwing a rock in space is more interesting and since we have the tech, I would like to see an astronaut actually do this to determine whether that rock will eventually came to a halt. the greater likelihood is space debris will impact its momentum, direction etc

@Veritasium , at 16:55 you said something along the lines "...since we don't have time symmetry, then energy - as we usually think of it - isn't conserved". But I don't see how this follows from what you shared as established at the time when you said that implication. What we agreed as established in the video (by that timestamp) is the following implication: "time translation symmetry => conservation of energy" but if the antecedent is false (time symmetry is false), we cannot conclude that the conclusion is false.

In other words, claiming that "...since we don't have time symmetry, then energy - as we usually think of it - isn't conserved" needs establishing that time symmetry is a NECESSARY condition for conservation of energy (i.e. conservation of energy => time symmetry,  or eq. Not time symmetry => Not conservation of energy).

If I'm missing something, someone please correct me.

